  <p class="kicker"><time datetime="2026-11-09">9 November 2026</time> · Essay · 5 min read</p>
  <h1>Has AI token usage plateaued? No. Spending has.</h1>
  <p class="deck">
    A team burned through its coding budget in weeks. The model was cheaper. The agents were longer. Online, people
    pointed at a falling chart and said demand had peaked. The chart was a price index.
  </p>

  <aside class="answer" aria-label="Short answer">
    <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> False on usage. Mostly true on spend, for now.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>The claim</h2>
  <p>AI token use has stopped growing. Demand has peaked. It shows up three ways.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The chart is rolling over.”</strong> In June 2026 a Citadel note on Silicon Data’s LLM Token Expenditure Index went around. The line was falling. Posts read it as a collapse in token demand.</li>
    <li><strong>“Even the big buyers are pulling back.”</strong> Uber used its full-year 2026 AI coding budget in about four months. Its COO said the payoff was hard to see. Microsoft reportedly cancelled most of its direct Claude Code licenses.</li>
    <li><strong>“Business spending has gone flat.”</strong> Ramp’s lead economist reported two straight months, August and September 2026, of almost no growth in business token spend.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why the claim is not silly</h2>
  <p>
    Growth looked like it slowed once before. Google reported about 480 trillion monthly tokens in May 2025, 980
    trillion in July, and 1.3 quadrillion in October. Monthly additions fell. Critics said the headline number hid a
    slowdown.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tokens measure compute, not customers. Reasoning models and agents burn more tokens per request. Totals can rise
    while the number of people using AI stays flat.
  </p>
  <p>
    New customers are getting scarce. Paid AI adoption among US businesses on Ramp reached 55.9% in September 2026, up
    0.6 points in a month. Ramp’s economist said it feels like the top of the S-curve. Growth has to come from heavier
    use, not more buyers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Money pays for data centres. Usage that brings in no extra revenue does not fund the next round of chips. Business
    token spend did stall in August and September.
  </p>

  <h2>What the numbers show</h2>
  <p>Volume is still rising. Price is falling. Spend stalled, then moved again.</p>

  <h3>Volume: still compounding</h3>
  <p>Google’s API traffic is the clearest volume number. Tokens per minute more than quadrupled in a year.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <caption>
      <strong>Google API tokens per minute, Q3 2025 to Q3 2026: 4.6×</strong>
      <span class="small muted">Billions of tokens per minute. Figures between points are growth over the prior quarter.</span>
    </caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quarter</th><th scope="col" class="num">Tokens per minute (bn)</th><th scope="col" class="num">Growth vs prior quarter</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q3 2025</th><td class="num">7</td><td class="num">—</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q4 2025</th><td class="num">10</td><td class="num">+43%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q1 2026</th><td class="num">16</td><td class="num">+60%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q2 2026</th><td class="num">22</td><td class="num">+38%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q3 2026</th><td class="num">32</td><td class="num">+45%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    The late-2025 slowdown did not last. In May 2026 Google said it was processing 3.2 quadrillion tokens a month
    across its products, seven times the year before.
  </p>
  <p>
    OpenRouter, a marketplace developers use to reach many models, shows the same shape. Weekly volume went from 0.5
    trillion tokens in January 2025 to 128.4 trillion in mid-September 2026. That is about 250 times higher. OpenRouter
    says agents overtook humans as the larger token users around 6 February 2026. One secondary report put late-October
    volume near 194 trillion a week. I could not open the original chart to confirm it.
  </p>

  <h3>Price: the chart that went viral</h3>
  <p>
    The index behind June’s panic measures the average price paid per million tokens, not how many tokens are used.
    Silicon Data said a better name would have been a price index. It read $0.85 per million tokens on 5 November 2026.
    It falls when buyers shift to cheaper models, even if they use more of them.
  </p>

  <h3>Spend: stalled, then moved</h3>
  <p>Businesses on Ramp are using more tokens and paying only a little more for them.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <caption class="small muted">Source: Ramp AI Index, firms using Ramp’s token spend product.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure (businesses on Ramp)</th><th scope="col" class="num">Week ending 23 Oct vs 25 Sep 2026</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Token volume</th><td class="num">+37.1%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Token spend</th><td class="num">+8.9%</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Blended price per million tokens (OpenAI and Anthropic)</th><td class="num">−26.8% ($0.76 to $0.56)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>
    Ramp recorded almost no growth in token spend in August and September. Early October hit a new peak, 1.5% above
    August. The latest week is up 8.9% on the month before. Its economist points to price cuts at the frontier and
    cheaper models getting better. Uber and Microsoft are not pure cost cuts. Uber’s COO questioned the value, and
    Microsoft reportedly cancelled licenses. Those are cuts in use, not just price shopping. The median firm paying for
    AI spent $15.70 per employee a month in September. The top 1% spent $9,567.53.
  </p>

  <h2>What this means on a desk</h2>
  <p>
    Cheaper tokens do not mean a smaller bill when the agent multiplies the calls. The invoice is price times volume.
    Most teams budget only the first. I have seen a weekly coding budget disappear in days because the model was
    cheaper and the loop was longer.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the spend stalls and the vendor tightens the key, the work that depended on the model stops. That is the same
    test as <a href="https://svikrant.medium.com/ai-proposes-a-human-authorizes-b64a10734bea">the folder</a>. If
    turning the model off breaks the process, the process was the model. A budget wall is just another way the model
    goes down.
  </p>
  <p>
    The volume rise is not free. It fills the review queue faster. The method for that does not change: sample, check
    the cheap facts, put a person only on the step you cannot undo. The token chart does not fix the ratio.
  </p>

  <h2>Verdict</h2>
  <p><strong>False on usage. Mostly true on spend, for now.</strong></p>
  <p>
    Token use has not plateaued. Google’s quarter-by-quarter API growth, OpenRouter, and Ramp all show volume still
    rising. What stalled was how much businesses paid. Prices per token fell about as fast as usage rose, so the bill
    flattened in August and September. Early October set a new peak and the latest week is up again. Most “plateau”
    posts still read a price chart or a spend chart as a usage chart.
  </p>

  <h2>What would change the verdict</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Google’s API tokens per minute stay flat or fall for two quarters in a row.</li>
    <li>OpenRouter’s weekly volume goes sideways for two months or more, outside of promotions ending.</li>
    <li>Ramp’s business token volume stops growing, not just spend.</li>
    <li>The big cloud providers cut their AI capital spending plans.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One limit: tokens measure compute, not value. If agents get more efficient through caching or shorter reasoning,
    token counts could flatten while real use grows. That would make tokens a worse yardstick, not a sign that demand
    had peaked.
  </p>

